sample_delivery_exceptions::challenger::.text:
{
  "findings": [
    {
      "question": "claims_supported",
      "verdict": "fails",
      "explanation": "The load-bearing claim C3 — 'Customers independently report entering the unit number at checkout and it not appearing on the package' — is cited to three feedback rows, but only ONE of them mentions checkout entry (EV-efad4673). EV-b26a6ee3 says only that the unit number was missing from the package; EV-6750be5d says the driver could not find unit 4B and the label showed only the street number. Neither establishes that the customer supplied the unit number. The support statement's phrase 'multiple post-exception comments describe entering the unit number at checkout' therefore overstates the record: n=1 for the checkout half of the mechanism. Second, C1's attribution clause rests on a free-text annotation in a ticket-summary row, 'missing or wrong unit number dominates' — 'wrong' unit numbers are consistent with customer input error, not internal truncation, so the same quote cited for internal loss equally supports the rival hypothesis. Third, C4 ('documented cause ... at scale') is drawn from a 200-delivery manual review in one metro area whose own record disclaims any measured effect; the evidence-claim set classifies that same material as an assumption (C-015), not a fact.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-efad4673",
          "quote": "My apartment number is never included even though I entered it at checkout."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-b26a6ee3",
          "quote": "Third time the unit number was missing from the package."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-6750be5d",
          "quote": "Driver said he could not find unit 4B. The address label only shows the street number."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-7ee8d3af",
          "quote": "missing or wrong unit number dominates - synthetic"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-47eef27a",
          "quote": "No follow-up measurement was taken, so the effect of correction on first-attempt success is unknown."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "contradictions_considered",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The draft engages the known contradiction list well (stale 91%, segment scope, notification pilot, vendor AI claim). But it drops evidence that cuts against its own mechanism. (a) The apartment address_error note says 'missing or wrong' — the 'wrong' half points at customer input, which is ALT-05/ALT-06 territory, not ALT-04; this ambiguity is never addressed. (b) At least one apartment respondent reports the address was correct and the failure was building navigation, an exception that would be miscounted as an address problem by ticket taxonomy. (c) Within apartments, access_issue (30.1%) plus driver_unable_to_locate (17.8%) together exceed address_error (37.3%), so 'largest single category' is not the same as 'largest addressable cause' — the draft asserts the former and reasons as if it had shown the latter. (d) The category label for address_error is 'incomplete or incorrect address', which does not distinguish loss-in-transit from bad capture.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-1fa987dd",
          "quote": "Address was right but the building has two entrances and the driver used the wrong one."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-43e8ff55",
          "quote": "exception_category: driver_unable_to_locate"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-af647d0f",
          "quote": "share_of_segment_percent: 30.1"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-a568696e",
          "quote": "incomplete or incorrect address - synthetic"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "preference_as_evidence",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "Stakeholder opinion is handled correctly and explicitly (the 'clearly worked' claim and the VP's AI push are both marked as opinion contradicted by the record). The residual problem is a different kind of unearned authority: an unsourced analyst annotation in a support-ops CSV row is being read as a causal finding about why apartment deliveries fail. Nobody has shown how that note was derived, and no telemetry on secondary-address-line capture exists (ALT-04's own product_usage is 'cannot_assess'). An annotation is at best an assumption; here it carries most of the diagnostic weight alongside a single customer comment.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-7ee8d3af",
          "quote": "note: missing or wrong unit number dominates - synthetic"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-986b01eb",
          "quote": "Notifications clearly worked last time. We saw the numbers move. We should just turn them on everywhere."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "non_ai_considered",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "Non-AI options dominate the option set and most are assessed in detail, so this is not a token listing. But the comparative paragraph that justifies selecting ALT-04 omits ALT-09 entirely — a non-AI, non-driver-app option aimed at the largest exception category overall (31.1%) and 30.1% within apartments. It is never argued against, merely absent. ALT-05 is also dismissed on a ground that applies to only half of it: the 'no driver-side consumer this cycle' objection defeats the access-notes field, not the required unit field, and the required unit field is precisely the remedy if the spike finds the loss is at customer input rather than internal. Dismissing ALT-05 wholesale removes the natural hedge against the recommendation's main failure mode.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-17784e47",
          "quote": "share_of_segment_percent: 31.1"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-af647d0f",
          "quote": "share_of_segment_percent: 30.1"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-15beacab",
          "quote": "No change to driver-facing screens can ship before the lock lifts."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "no_build_considered",
      "verdict": "passes",
      "explanation": "ALT-01 is argued as a real baseline with an observed and worsening cost, and ALT-03 is argued as the honest fallback; the recommendation's own conditions in effect make the first committed spend a diagnostic. One caution rather than a defect: naming the recommendation 'ALT-04' before the localisation spike reports pre-commits the quarter's headline to a fix whose premise the spike exists to test. If the spike is genuinely decision-shaping, the decision being approved this cycle is ALT-03-shaped, and the write-up should say so.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-ef605f46",
          "quote": "metric: support_contacts_per_1k_deliveries\nvalue: 38.2"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-15beacab",
          "quote": "Integration effort has not been estimated."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "overconfident",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "'Moderate' overstates what the record earns. Three of the four links in the chain are unmeasured: (1) that the secondary address line is lost internally rather than never captured — one customer comment, no capture telemetry, and a source note that says 'missing or wrong'; (2) that repairing addresses improves first-attempt success — never measured; (3) effort and elapsed time — not estimated anywhere. In addition the prioritisation reasons from support-ticket shares as if they were exception-cause shares, while the exception-to-contact ratio by category is itself listed as missing evidence, and no cost per exception exists to size the payoff. Support should read low until the spike returns; the ticket-share and segment-gap facts are solid, but they support 'investigate apartment address data', not 'the defect is internal and repairing it will move the metric'.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-47eef27a",
          "quote": "No follow-up measurement was taken, so the effect of correction on first-attempt success is unknown."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-15beacab",
          "quote": "Integration effort has not been estimated."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-8adfb0a2",
          "quote": "synthetic - not measured; field exists but was never populated"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "what_could_make_it_wrong",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The draft's own 'what would change it' list is good but misses two specific, plausible failure modes. First: the spike finds label fidelity is already high and the dominant sub-cause inside apartment address_error is the 'wrong' half of 'missing or wrong' — customers typing an incorrect or absent unit number at checkout. Then there is no internal defect to repair, ALT-04 dissolves, and the quarter's capacity has been spent on a spike plus a warehouse-latency slice. Second: address_error ticket share is a share of contacts, not of failed deliveries. If apartment address problems generate contacts at a higher rate than access failures do (plausible, since a missing unit number produces a redelivery conversation), then 37.3% of tickets overstates 37.3% of first-attempt failures, and fixing it will move the contact metric far more than the 93% first-attempt goal — which is the goal the recommendation is sold against. The exception-to-contact ratio by category is an acknowledged gap in the evidence set and is never reconciled with the use made of the ticket table.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-7ee8d3af",
          "quote": "missing or wrong unit number dominates - synthetic"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-a568696e",
          "quote": "ticket_count: 1510"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-92455949",
          "quote": "Raise first-attempt delivery success to 93% by the end of FY26."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "what_to_test",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The proposed test bundles the diagnosis and the cure into one hypothesis and so cannot fail cleanly. Before any build money: (1) a label-fidelity audit — sample apartment orders and compare the secondary address line as captured at checkout against the value on the generated label, giving the loss rate directly, which no evidence currently contains; (2) a capture-rate baseline — what share of apartment orders arrive at checkout completion with a non-empty secondary line at all, which separates internal loss from input gap and is the pivot between ALT-04 and ALT-05/ALT-06; (3) a re-classification of a sample of apartment address_error tickets into 'we lost it' versus 'customer never gave it' versus 'customer gave it wrong'; (4) a randomised address-correction follow-up to put a number on the effect on first-attempt success, absent since 2024; (5) the exception-to-contact ratio by category, so ticket shares stop standing in for delivery-failure shares. Conversion effect only needs testing if the answer turns out to be a checkout form change.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-47eef27a",
          "quote": "A manual review of 200 failed deliveries in one metro area found that 41% had an incomplete or incorrect delivery address."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-15beacab",
          "quote": "Delivery exception events land in the warehouse with a lag of up to 24 hours."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "reclassify": [
    {
      "claim_id": "C3",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "The evidential half ('customers report the unit number missing from labels') is a fact for a handful of respondents, but the conclusion 'which points to an internal data defect rather than customer input error' is an inference resting on one comment (EV-efad4673) that mentions checkout entry. It is the pivotal assumption of the whole recommendation and should be labelled as one, not as a fact."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "C1",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "The counts and share (921 tickets, 37.3%) are fact. The attribution clause — 'the ticket notes attribute it specifically to missing or wrong unit numbers' — is an unsourced annotation in a CSV row, and it says 'wrong' as well as 'missing', so it does not establish internal loss. Split the claim, or classify the causal part as an assumption."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "C4",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "Labelled fact but derives entirely from a 200-delivery manual review in one metro area with no follow-up measurement; the evidence-claim set already classifies this same material as an assumption (C-015). Carrying it as a fact in the recommendation inflates the apparent base of support."
    }
  ],
  "recommended_support": "low",
  "what_would_change_it": [
    "A label-fidelity audit showing the secondary address line is present on labels for nearly all apartment orders where it was captured at checkout — i.e. no internal loss to repair, and the 'missing or wrong' note means 'wrong'.",
    "A capture-rate baseline showing a material share of apartment orders never carry a secondary address line out of checkout, which makes this an input-capture problem (ALT-05/ALT-06) rather than a data-loss problem (ALT-04).",
    "An exception-to-contact ratio showing apartment address problems generate contacts at a higher rate than other categories, meaning the 37.3% ticket share overstates their share of first-attempt failures and the fix moves the contact goal but not the 93% goal.",
    "A ticket re-classification finding that a large fraction of apartment address_error tickets are building-navigation failures like the two-entrance case, which belong to access work rather than address work.",
    "An engineering estimate showing the repair sits in the carrier or driver path, which is unshippable this cycle."
  ],
  "what_to_test": [
    "Label-fidelity audit: for a sample of apartment orders, compare the secondary address line captured at checkout with the value printed on the delivery label, and report the loss rate.",
    "Checkout capture rate for the secondary address line on apartment orders — the currently missing telemetry that separates internal loss from input gap.",
    "Manual re-classification of a sample of apartment address_error tickets into lost-internally / never-supplied / supplied-incorrectly / actually-access.",
    "Randomised follow-up to the 2024 address-correction spot-check to put a measured number on the effect of correction on first-attempt success.",
    "Exception-to-support-contact ratio by category and segment, so ticket shares are not used as proxies for delivery-failure shares.",
    "Cost of an exception (support handling time plus redelivery) so the spike-plus-build spend can be compared with the benefit it is meant to buy."
  ]
}

